Regarding the R–I–T relationship, my focus is on the intermediary and the diversity and complexity surrounding it. I retain one entry per intermediary per act and allow multiple values for regulator_context and target_context. These capture the range of institutional contexts, but they do not, by themselves, specify which actors participate in each activity. I would therefore be interested in examples where the existing descriptions leave these distinctions unclear, while retaining the intermediary as the unit of observation. 

Your suggestion about the legal basis for identifying intermediaries concerns a different question from my recognition variable, which records how an intermediary is approved or selected and therefore belongs to the regulation of intermediaries. My coding, for example, already accommodates structural reliance, although in a different manner. Insurance companies illustrate this: an insurance requirement provides a basis for identifying their risk-underwriting function without expressly assigning them a regulatory role or even naming them. Their recognition pathway is coded as “None” because the act specifies no approval or selection pathway, while the description explains its reliance on insurance. The act also does not specify how insurers themselves are regulated. Although other EU or national legislation may regulate them, I only incorporate those requirements when the act refers to that legislation. The question is therefore whether recording the basis for identification separately would add useful information beyond these descriptions. I would want to clarify the distinctions and possible overlap between direct assignment, legal recognition, and structural reliance before adopting that classification.

On traceability, I provide several rationale fields that explain coding decisions with references to specific provisions, alongside the relevant-provisions field, which identifies all provisions relevant to the intermediary. If you have examples where the connection between a decision and its supporting provision remains unclear, these would help me improve the documentation.

My codebook also distinguishes functions from mechanisms and allows multiple functions to be coded for the same intermediary. This accommodates overlapping activities, but does not necessarily resolve difficulties in distinguishing the categories. Under my restrictive coding approach, an actor must perform at least one regulatory function to qualify as an intermediary. If an actor performs such a function that the existing categories cannot capture, I would consider whether an additional function category is needed.
 
