Dear Rotem and David,

Rotem, thank you for your thoughtful and detailed response. Your clarifications have helped me reconsider some of my earlier comments, particularly the distinction between the identification of intermediaries and their recognition pathways.

I think our approaches may be more complementary than my initial observations suggested.

I agree that retaining the intermediary × act as the primary unit of observation is important. My concern is not necessarily to replace this structure with individual R–I–T relationships, but to examine whether an additional, provision-linked analytical layer could preserve distinctions between specific actors, activities, and recipients.

For example, when the same intermediary performs different activities involving multiple regulators and targets, multivalued context fields may correctly identify the institutional environment without necessarily allowing us to reconstruct each specific relationship. I think this could be tested without changing the primary unit of observation.

Your insurance example was also particularly helpful. I agree that the legal basis for identifying an intermediary is conceptually different from the approval or selection pathway captured by your recognition variable. What I would like to explore is whether recording the identification basis in a standardized way adds comparative value beyond the existing narrative descriptions, especially in cases of structural reliance.

Regarding traceability, my concern is less about the availability of provision references and more about whether an independent coder can reconstruct the inferential link between a particular provision and each classification decision.

Our recent structural extraction pilot may offer useful diagnostic material for these questions. We completed 19 human-adjudicated decisions, which highlighted issues involving compound relationships, actor–action–recipient alignment, legal effects, and conditional normative structures. These findings concern our extraction procedure rather than constituting an independent validation of your codebook.

I would be very happy to meet over Zoom and work through a small selection of cases together. I suggest preparing a short comparative matrix beforehand, using your existing coding categories alongside our provision-level relational extraction, with particular attention to assurance, auditing, monitoring, evaluation, and transmission.

I believe this could help us distinguish genuine conceptual disagreements from differences in analytical granularity and identify where clarification is sufficient and where additional information might be useful.

Thank you again. I look forward to developing this work together.

Best wishes,
Igor

Minha avaliação final
Existe uma oportunidade científica interessante nessa correspondência: Rotem está priorizando a comparabilidade entre intermediários; nosso trabalho está investigando a integridade das relações jurídicas que fundamentam sua identificação e atuação.

Esses dois objetivos não precisam competir.

Eu utilizaria a reunião para investigar uma questão central:

Can we preserve the intermediary as the primary unit of observation without losing the relational information embedded in individual legal provisions?

Essa é uma pergunta capaz de conectar nosso trabalho empírico à discussão mais ampla sobre arquiteturas de direito e governança que David está propondo. Mais importante: é uma pergunta testável.

